---
<DocsLayout title="Joins" description="Join tables in thinDB with equality keys, ranges, and general ON conditions." headings={headings}>
  <PageHeader eyebrow="SQL guide" title="Joins" description="Combine columnar relations with inner, outer, cross, and natural joins. Equality keys drive hash and merge execution; any other ON condition is checked per candidate pair. thinDB selects from several local algorithms without a distributed planner." />
  <h2 id="join-types">Join types</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>SQL form</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td><code>INNER JOIN</code> / <code>JOIN</code></td><td>Pairs of rows that satisfy the <code>ON</code> condition.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>LEFT [OUTER] JOIN</code></td><td>All left rows, null-extending unmatched right columns.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>RIGHT [OUTER] JOIN</code></td><td>All right rows, null-extending unmatched left columns.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>FULL [OUTER] JOIN</code></td><td>All matched and unmatched rows from both sides.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>CROSS JOIN</code>, <code>FROM a, b</code></td><td>The Cartesian product. Equalities in <code>WHERE</code> between comma-joined sources become join keys.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>JOIN ... USING (c, ...)</code>, <code>NATURAL JOIN</code></td><td>An equality join on the named (or all shared) columns. Each shared column appears once: the left value for inner and left joins, the right value for right joins, and <code>COALESCE(left, right)</code> for full joins.</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>

  <h2 id="equality-joins">Equality joins</h2>
  <CodeBlock language="sql" code={`SELECT i.invoice_id, c.name, i.amount
FROM invoices AS i
JOIN customers AS c
  ON i.customer_id = c.customer_id
 AND i.division_id = c.division_id;

SELECT c.customer_id
FROM customers c
LEFT JOIN invoices i ON c.customer_id = i.customer_id
WHERE i.invoice_id IS NULL;

SELECT * FROM invoices JOIN customers USING (customer_id);`} />
  <p>Composite equality keys are supported, and keys of different numeric, text, temporal, or nested types compare at their common type. Qualify columns in multi-table queries so each key resolves to one side.</p>

  <h2 id="general-on">General ON conditions</h2>
  <CodeBlock language="sql" code={`SELECT e.event_id, p.price
FROM events e
LEFT JOIN prices p
  ON e.product_id = p.product_id
 AND e.occurred_at >= p.valid_from
 AND e.occurred_at < p.valid_to;

SELECT a.id, b.id
FROM accounts a
JOIN accounts b ON a.parent_id = b.id OR a.alias_of = b.id;`} />
  <p>An <code>ON</code> clause may hold ranges, <code>&lt;&gt;</code>, <code>OR</code>, expressions over both inputs, or just <code>TRUE</code>, in inner and outer joins. Top-level <code>left.col = right.col</code> conjuncts still become join keys. The rest of the condition is a residual: an inner join filters on it after matching keys, and an outer join checks it for each candidate pair, so a left row whose candidates all fail is null-extended exactly as the SQL standard requires.</p>

  <h2 id="execution">Execution behavior</h2>
  <p>The execution layer includes hash join, sort-merge join, nested-loop join, and range-sweep join. The <code>auto</code> route uses manifest statistics and can respond to skew. Algorithm choice is internal physical execution, not a semantic SQL rewrite.</p>
  <ul><li>Order keys, segment statistics, and key Bloom filters can reduce scan work before the join.</li><li>Hash execution is effective for ordinary equality joins that fit the query budget.</li><li>Sort-merge provides a bounded path for ordered inputs and skewed keys.</li><li>An outer join with a residual condition runs the nested-loop join over candidate pairs taken from a key index on the right input. With no equality key at all, every pair is a candidate, so keep at least one equality key on large inputs.</li></ul>

  <h2 id="limitations">Current limitations</h2>
  <p><code>SEMI JOIN</code> and <code>ANTI JOIN</code> syntax is not implemented. Express semi and anti joins with <code>EXISTS</code>/<code>NOT EXISTS</code> or <code>IN</code>/<code>NOT IN</code> subqueries. Join hints such as <code>[broadcast]</code> or <code>[shuffle]</code> are not accepted.</p>
  <PageLinks previous={{ label: 'Query data', href: '/sql/queries/' }} next={{ label: 'Window functions', href: '/sql/windows/' }} />
</DocsLayout>
